le I'd like to tell you about one  man's story who really inspires me  siddhartha gautama was a wealthy prince  who lived over 2,500 years ago in the  foothills of the Himalayan mountains as  the story goes he had every imaginable  luxury he lived a sheltered life in his  castle until one day when he was about  our age he goes out on a carriage ride  and he removes the veil and he sees true  suffering in the world for the first  time he ends up going back to his palace  and all of those luxuries he previously  enjoyed now seemed hollow fleeting  pleasures incapable of bringing true  fulfillment so what does he do he  actually goes out into the world he  leaves it all behind and he deprives  himself of absolutely everything but  this too does not bring fulfillment so  he struggles and eventually he finds  this middle  way between excess and deprivation and  it's on this middle way that he comes to  know enlightenment and he earns the  title of the Buddha enlightenment  fulfillment that's why we're all here  right well notice that the Buddha didn't  get it by going back to his palace and  by writing a check and continuing his  own life of luxury he couldn't do it he  dug deeper than his own pocketbook and  he recognized it's not about how much we  give it's about how fully we give how  completely we give of ourselves the  Buddha chose life over luxury so I know  what everyone in here must be thinking  right now at this point whoa  total buzzkill debbie downer of the  stage well actually I'm not here to make  anybody feel guilty in fact the exact  opposite this has brought this approach  to life it's brought me an unimaginable  amount of joy sacred meaning and purpose  you know rather than seeking out  fulfillment by trying to climb higher  and higher up this wealth and status  ladder then with Jose at my side I've  hopped off the ladder now like a roof  over my head a hot shower a bed a full  stomach these things are seen for the  global luxuries they actually are an  abundant gratitude emerges from  simplicity what I'd like to ask each and  every one of you to do to do is this  travel to the developing world if you  haven't already go to the developing  world and create a meaningful loving  authentic relationship with someone in  extreme poverty in the developing world  remove the veil step out of your comfort  zone and turn these people from  statistics into your brothers and  sisters it's through their friendship  that will come to realize you know  recognize our own love  juries are on consumption choices even  our own goals begin to transform and our  brothers and sisters will help us to  find the middle way and it's out of love  for them we'll choose life over luxury  ultimately  it's not about us saving Jose and those  in the developing world it's about them  saving us
 

